  "description": "This is a short note on the new addition to the USA portfolio…",
  "body_html": "<p>This is a short note on the new addition to the USA portfolio…</p><div class=\"paywall-jump\" data-component-name=\"PaywallToDOM\"></div><p></p><div><hr></div><p><strong>Trade Alert:</strong> Buy <strong>WFC</strong> for USA portfolio.</p><div><hr></div><p>Wells Fargo (WFC) stands out as a massive deposit-gathering machine, holding the third-highest share in the United States right behind JPMorgan Chase and Bank of America. That alone gives it a cost advantage since cheap deposit funding can power future loan growth and margins. The bank also carries a wide moat, showing that despite past missteps, its core businesses—particularly the middle-market commercial space and wealth advisory network—remain tough to replicate.</p><p>Sure, it’s still under an asset cap, but WFC has already notched some progress with regulators, and the eventual lifting of that cap could let it chase additional growth. Leadership seems determined to trim expenses, aiming to push the efficiency ratio under 60%. That means more profit per dollar of revenue if they can keep the top line steady or expand. Plus, it helps that they’ve got one of the most recognized banking brands out there—despite the reputation dents, the everyday customer still trusts Wells for mortgages, checking accounts, and small-business banking. Sprinkle in ongoing investment in credit cards, middle-market investment banking, and an expanding wealth franchise, and there’s a lot of irons in the fire.</p><p>From a broader perspective, higher interest rates have boosted banks’ net interest income, and WFC is built to benefit as rates stay elevated (and if they come down more slowly than expected, it could still capitalize on loan growth). Throw in that it has the lowest global systemically important bank surcharge of the Big Four, and you can see how WFC might crank out returns that comfortably beat its cost of capital over the long haul.</p>",
  "has_dynamic_content": false,
